@startuml class-diagram
' Where the business rules live. Models are plain data; every rule that
' matters sits in a service, so no controller can bypass it.
title Intelligent ERP — Business logic (service layer)

skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam ArrowColor #6B7684
skinparam class {
  BorderColor #9AA5B1
  BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
}
skinparam package {
  BorderColor #C3CCD6
  FontStyle bold
}

package "Stock & documents" #F4FBF6 {
  class StockService {
    recordMovement()
    --
    Every quantity change is a
    movement. Runs in a locked
    transaction, refuses to go
    below zero.
  }

  class DocumentService {
    confirmSale()
    receivePurchase()
    cancelSale()
    nextNumber()
    --
    Moves documents through their
    lifecycle and triggers stock
    and accounting together.
  }

  class InventoryValuationService {
    registerReceipt()
    unitCost()
    --
    Weighted-average (AVCO) cost.
    Each receipt blends in; sales
    relieve stock at that average,
    so the books track real cost.
  }
}

package "Accounting" #FBF4F4 {
  class AccountingService {
    post()
    postSaleConfirmed()
    postPurchaseReceived()
    postPosSale()
    reverseSale()
    trialBalance()
    --
    Refuses any entry where
    debit does not equal credit.
    POS, sales and receipts all
    post here at moving-average cost.
  }

  class AccountMap {
    code(key)
    --
    Turns "cheques_receivable"
    into the account the company
    actually configured.
  }
}

package "Sales, pricing & sharing" #F7F4FB {
  class PricingService {
    priceFor(product, qty, customer)
    --
    Resolves the price from the
    customer's pricelist (or the
    default): most specific rule,
    then highest quantity break.
  }

  class VendorBillService {
    record()
    matchReport()
    approve()
    --
    3-way match: bill vs order vs
    receipt. Anything off is an
    exception a manager clears.
  }

  class OnlinePaymentService {
    initiate()
    confirm()
    --
    Customer pays a shared sale via
    a pluggable gateway (sandbox by
    default); confirm posts to bank.
  }

  class TunisianPayrollTax {
    compute(gross, family)
    --
    CNSS, IRPP (progressive) and
    CSS from editable settings —
    real gross-to-net for Tunisia.
  }
}

package "Treasury (Tunisia)" #F2F8FB {
  class InstrumentService {
    receive() / issue()
    deposit()
    clear()
    bounce()
    --
    Cheques and traites.
    Each step posts its own
    entry and records history.
  }

  class InstallmentService {
    createPlan()
    applyPayment()
    markOverdue()
    customerCredit()
    --
    "Khlas bel taqsit".
    Last instalment absorbs
    the rounding.
  }

  class PaymentService {
    record()
    settleInstallment()
    --
    Cash and transfers post now.
    Cheques wait until they clear.
  }

  class ReconciliationService {
    parseCsv()
    import()
    suggestions()
    match()
    report()
    --
    Matching says "this bank line
    IS that payment" — it does
    not post again.
  }
}

package "Access & audit" #F4F8FB {
  class PermissionService {
    has(user, key)
    canOn(user, record)
    fieldAccess()
    --
    User grant beats role,
    deny beats allow,
    restrictions never inherit.
  }

  class "EnsureRole /\nEnsureModuleAccess\n(request gates)" as gates {
    role gate
    module allowlist gate
    --
    Run on every request after
    sign-in. Super admin passes any
    gate; a custom role may touch only
    the modules on its allowlist —
    reads and writes alike.
  }

  class "Modules (catalogue)" as modules {
    keys()
    forSegment(path)
    --
    One list of the app's modules,
    shared by the role editor, the
    API gate and the sidebar, so all
    three agree on what a module is.
  }

  class AuditService {
    record()
    asAgent()
    batch()
    --
    Who, when, before, after.
    Never breaks the operation
    it is watching.
  }
}

gates --> modules : which module\nis this path?

' ---- who calls whom ----
DocumentService --> StockService : moves stock
DocumentService --> AccountingService : posts entries
InstrumentService --> AccountingService : posts entries
InstrumentService --> InstallmentService : settles / reopens
PaymentService --> AccountingService : posts entries
PaymentService --> InstallmentService : updates schedule
ReconciliationService --> InstrumentService : clears on match

DocumentService --> InventoryValuationService : blends receipt cost
AccountingService --> InventoryValuationService : values COGS
OnlinePaymentService --> AccountingService : posts on confirm

' Every posting goes through AccountingService, so one arrow says it all —
' drawing one per caller just added long curves across the diagram.
AccountingService --> AccountMap : which account?

note bottom of AccountMap
  This is why no Tunisian
  accounting rule is hardcoded:
  the account is a setting.
end note

@enduml
